Lab 4 README

Subset of lab3: 
    The subset of lab3 for this was maintaining all existing functionality and having a generally similar website structure (through HTML and CSS) to that of lab3. For example, font sizes, fonts, font type, colors, and button sizes may not match.

Worklog: 

    As I began the lab, I decided that giving ChatGPT everything at once would we much too difficult for it. I gave it instructions on how to make a basic replica of my lab3 navbar. I then had to provide it my file structure so that the pages would link correctly. Next, I provided it a description of the rest of the basic page layout, such as where to display data. After several attempts of correcting the AI on the page layout, I instructed it on how to create the popup form layout for the manual data input. It made a beautiful popup on the first try but ended up completely ruining the rest of the normal page layout. After a long set of back and forth prompts it accomplished its goal. I then instructed it on how to create an SQL file that created and handled the database structure. The AI did this perfectly, so I then instructed it to create a handling of the PokeAPI data, which included calling the API, storing it in the data tables using PHP, fetching it from the data tables using PHP, and displaying it dynamically. This was my first major roadblock using the AI. After fixing some basic CSS and linking issues, I repeatedly tried to explain to the AI that it needed to not only be able to display the data on startup, but also whenever a button was clicked. Eventually after explaining the approach it various methods it figured it out. I then had to instruct the AI on how to fetch the weatherAPI data using the HTML Geolocation API. Despite being in concept similar to the fetching of the PokeAPI information, the AI struggled with creating correct PHP code, correct Javascript code, and continued to attempt to modify existing code that was unrelated to the task at hand. On many occassions I had to specificy that it could not modify unrelated code bits and had to display the entire file's code. ChatGPT now seems to give users a limited number of free uses for its more advanced model before running into a cooldown period, where it begins to switch to a dumber model. I ran into this at several points, which could explain why I kept running into roadblocks that didn't seem to make sense based on what the AI was able to do just a few prompts prior. After it completed the weatherAPI information fetching, I needed it to modify the code to allow manual editing by the user of the displayed information. The AI was able to complete this with a minor mistake, which I recognized after reviewing the code and instructed it on how to fix it. It would also randomly swap indexes of items in arrays that it would generate, which took me some time to notice and instruct on how to fix. The last major functionality to tackle was the weatherAPI search functionality. Users needed to be able to search a location and have that information be displayed and override any existing information. It generated some code which I could tell had some structural problems so I informed the AI of these issues. I also had the AI create a background image of clouds and after 2 attempts found one that I liked. I told it to put it as the background among other CSS adjustments which went fairly smoothly. Finally, I provided it a few more instructions on basic CSS changes to enhance the look of the website and did a code snippet at the end. I needed it to update the password and username of the PHP files to access the database on my VM, so I told it how to do that and copied this code over the old part in each PHP file.

